title: Gates are words
check: behaves
---- intro
In VHDL the gates are words: `not`, `and`, `or`, `xor`, `nand`. You write them between signals, much as you would in a sentence:

- `y <= not a;` is the NOT gate
- `y <= a and b;` is the AND gate
- `y <= a or b;` is OR

This entity has two inputs, `a` and `b`. Watch the drawing on the right change as you type.
---- task
Write the AND gate from the Circuits lessons: `y` is on only when both `a` and `b` are on.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity and_gate is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    b : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end and_gate;

architecture rtl of and_gate is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity and_gate is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    b : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end and_gate;

architecture rtl of and_gate is
begin
    y <= a and b;
end rtl;
---- hint1
It is one line, like `y <= a;`, with the gate's word between the two inputs.
---- hint2
`y <= a and b;`
---- solution
`y <= a and b;` is an AND gate with `a` and `b` as its inputs and `y` as its output. Click the switches on the right to try all four combinations.
---- done
Try changing `and` to `or` and watch the gate in the drawing change, then put it back. The text and the circuit are the same thing.
